  "transcript": "# The dual role of the Lord Advocate\n\n## What the Lord Advocate does\n> The office combines ministerial legal advice and independent prosecution functions, and that mix is seen as both practical and complex.\n\nChair: Please introduce yourself and the role you held.\nJames Wolffe: I was Lord Advocate from 2016 to 2021. The office combines being the Government’s senior law officer and head of Scotland’s prosecution system. That combination can be organised differently, but it carries trade-offs.\nJames Wolffe: Legally, the Lord Advocate holds independent prosecutorial powers under the Scotland Act, sits as a Scottish Minister, advises Government on law under the ministerial code, and sometimes handles other ministerial functions, like civil asset recovery.\n\n\n## Advantages of the combined role\n> Having one senior lawyer in Government gives a single voice on legal and prosecution policy, direct accountability to Parliament, and practical control over resources.\n\nChair: What are the main advantages of combining the law‑officer and prosecutor roles?\nJames Wolffe: First, it gives ministerial oversight of the whole prosecution system that is accountable to the Scottish Parliament. Scotland has a single public prosecution service; that matters.\nJames Wolffe: Second, a Lord Advocate can set prosecution policy across the system and negotiate the Crown’s budget, which lets them fix issues—like resourcing death investigations—across the whole service.\nJames Wolffe: Third, being in Government gives a strong voice for rule‑of‑law considerations when Ministers make policy, especially on criminal justice.\nKirsteen Sullivan: Would the Lord Advocate be involved in policy that raises legal concerns, for example if a proposed scheme might breach legal limits?\nJames Wolffe: Yes. Good policy needs early legal input. Government lawyers do most of that work; law officers step in for particularly difficult competence or legal‑risk issues and can call matters in when needed.\n\n\n## Risks, safeguards and public confidence\n> Independence is protected by law, politics, professional standards and institutional practice — but public perception of a conflict is the main current worry.\n\nKenneth Stevenson: What are the main risks from the dual role?\nJames Wolffe: The prime risk is public misunderstanding: people may not see how the prosecutorial independence is protected when the Lord Advocate also sits in Government.\nJames Wolffe: There are legal safeguards (Scotland Act duties), political safeguards (Parliamentary accountability), personal safeguards (senior lawyers’ integrity), and institutional safeguards (Crown Office, advocate deputes and the Solicitor General).\nDave Doogan: If we split the roles, could we lose anything important?\nJames Wolffe: Splitting retains operational independence but risks weakening the law officer’s voice inside Government. The law officer helps Ministers navigate legal limits and keep the Government lawful.\nChair: Is public perception a separate problem from institutional independence?\nJames Wolffe: Yes. An institution can be independent in reality but still lose public confidence. If confidence is genuinely broken, structural change might be needed. If it’s a perception issue, clearer arrangements could fix it.\n\n\n## Cabinet attendance and communications\n> Law officers being at Cabinet is normal for giving legal advice, but frequent attendance and certain communications in high‑profile cases have fuelled concern about perception.\n\nDouglas McAllister: You attended Cabinet more often than other Lord Advocates. Did that relate to major events like Brexit, covid or the Salmond matter?\nJames Wolffe: I attended frequently during an extraordinary five years. The purpose was to ensure legal issues were fully considered when Ministers decided policy. That is a legitimate safeguard.\nDouglas McAllister: Does frequent Cabinet attendance make the public more suspicious about independence?\nJames Wolffe: I can only speak as a lawyer: having the law officer present strengthens law’s voice. But I accept reasonable people may see it differently, so perception needs attention.\nMr MacDonald: How often would you provide confidential updates to Ministers about live criminal cases?\nJames Wolffe: Not routinely. In my time I provided limited factual updates in exceptional cases so Ministers knew the legal position and constraints, for example to avoid Ministers saying something that might breach the Contempt of Court Act.\n\n\n## Recusal, Branchform and practical fixes\n> Recent controversial communications (Branchform) show the problem: ringfencing law officers from decisions but still telling them sensitive facts can look wrong; a clear protocol or handing minister updates to prosecutors could help.\n\nMr MacDonald: Was it appropriate for a recused law officer to send case updates to Ministers, or should a Crown prosecutor have done that?\nJames Wolffe: Where law officers are recused from decision making, they can still be given factual updates. But the Branchform exchanges highlighted how giving sensitive updates to Ministers via a law officer created perception problems.\nKirsteen Sullivan: Would asking an advocate depute or Crown Agent to send the update have avoided the perception issue?\nJames Wolffe: Probably yes. In hindsight, having the prosecutor or Crown Office send factual updates on the instructions of the advocate depute would have avoided unfair allegations of impropriety.\nDouglas McAllister: Does this show the dual role is unworkable?\nJames Wolffe: Not necessarily. You can address the problem narrowly: clearer published protocols, ringfencing law officers from operational communications, or creating a visible Lord Advocate office to explain how the roles fit together.\nChair: Are the Crown Office’s August 2026 practice statements new law or mainly codifying old practice?\nJames Wolffe: They largely reflect long‑standing practices about ringfencing law officers from decisions, but clearer rules and transparency about who communicates with Ministers would help public confidence.\n\n\n## Recruitment, political alignment and tenure\n> The Committee discussed how law officers are chosen, the benefits of appointing non‑party legal experts, and why Mr Wolffe left in 2021.\n\nKirsteen Sullivan: How were you chosen in 2016, and were you required to be non‑party?\nJames Wolffe: I was asked to meet the First Minister and then offered the post; I made clear I had no party affiliation. The role has been filled both by party‑aligned and non‑party lawyers historically.\nSusan Murray: Is it essential that law officers be career prosecutors?\nJames Wolffe: Not essential, but prosecutorial or criminal justice experience helps. I’d been an advocate depute for a time which made the transition easier, but law‑making and ministerial judgment are also important skills.\nKirsteen Sullivan: Why didn’t you seek to stay on in 2021?\nJames Wolffe: It wasn’t a question of being forced out. My mother’s ill health and having served through Brexit and covid made it the right time to step down.\nMaureen Burke: How did you divide work with the Solicitor General?\nJames Wolffe: We worked closely in Crown Office. There’s no fixed template; we shared tasks and each covered all parts when needed. That deputy role provides resilience.\nChair: Any final practical suggestions?\nJames Wolffe: Consider making the Lord Advocate’s combined constitutional role more visible, publish how recusals and communications are handled, and, for exceptional high‑profile cases, ensure prosecutors themselves handle ministerial updates to avoid perception problems.\n",
